 # Tree-based structures (Octree, Pointer Tree, Sparse Voxel DAG)
 
-Also called: Hierarchical Sparse Voxel Structures, SVO Family
+Also called: Sparse Voxel Trees, Hierarchical Voxel Structures, Voxel Hierarchies
 
 ## Overview
-Tree-based structures organize volumetric data into a recursive spatial subdivision, enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution by storing data only in non-empty regions [S2, 2792-octree-based-structures.md]. They set themselves apart from sibling structures like Hash Grids or Brick Maps by prioritizing hierarchical traversal and spatial locality over constant-time access or block-based storage, allowing for compression proportional to data sparsity [2792-octree-based-structures.md, 4983-sparse-voxel-tree-svo-tree.md].
+Tree-based structures represent volumetric data using recursive spatial subdivision, where empty or uniform regions are collapsed into single nodes, providing compression proportional to data sparsity [S2, 2792-octree-based-structures.md]. This family solves the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution [2792-octree-based-structures.md]. Unlike sibling structures like Hash Grids or Brick Maps, standard tree-based methods rely on explicit hierarchical traversal (pointers or indices) rather than hash lookups or fixed-size blocks, prioritizing spatial locality and topological flexibility over constant-time access or out-of-core simplicity [2792-octree-based-structures.md, 4983-sparse-voxel-tree-svo-tree.md].
 
