 ### [Adaptively Sampled Distance Fields](7773-adaptively-sampled-distance-fields.md)
-A representation for vector outlines and 3D shapes using tree compression on signed distance fields, modified to preserve the sign of the approximated data to accurately encode the zero set (shape boundary).
+Adaptively Sampled Distance Fields (ASDF) are a tree-based compression method for representing 3D shapes and vector outlines by encoding a signed distance field (SDF) rather than binary occupancy. Unlike traditional binary-valued trees that require fine refinement to capture smooth boundaries, ASDF exploits the smoothness of the distance function to achieve high compression ratios while preserving the topological accuracy of the shape's zero set. It is particularly suited for applications requiring resolution-independent antialiasing and magnification of complex vector outlines.
 
 #### NerfTree
-- **Definition:** A two-level octree structure called NerfTree, consisting of a coarse density voxel volume for approximate geometry caching and fine sparse voxel grids localized around the surface for refinement [S1].
-- **Step:** Use the coarse density voxel volume to localize the object's surface [S1]. Use the fine sparse voxel grids to refine geometry and radiance estimation in surface-adjacent regions [S1]. Progressively update the density voxel volumes during the training process [S1].
-- **Pitfall:** Introduces a trade-off between memory usage and precision arising from the voxel structure and density grid, resulting in a larger memory footprint [S1].
-- **Number:** Reduces training time by up to 88% compared to traditional NeRF [S1]. Improves inference speed to more than 200 fps [S1].
+**Definition**
+NerfTree is a two-level octree structure consisting of a coarse density voxel volume for approximate geometry caching and fine sparse voxel grids localized around the surface for refinement [S1].
+
+**Steps**
+1. Use the coarse density voxel volume to localize the object's surface [S1].
+2. Use the fine sparse voxel grids to refine geometry and radiance estimation in surface-adjacent regions [S1].
+3. Progressively update the density voxel volumes during the training process [S1].
+
+**Pitfalls**
+Introduces a trade-off between memory usage and precision arising from the voxel structure and density grid, resulting in a larger memory footprint [S1].
+
+**Numbers**
+Reduces training time by up to 88% compared to traditional NeRF and improves inference speed to more than 200 fps [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Tree-based sparse structures are inspired by classical computer graphics techniques such as the Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) [RW80] and the Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) [LK10], which model a scene in a sparse hierarchical form to accelerate ray tracing [S1]. The SVO was introduced by Laine and Karras in 2010 [LK10, cited in S1]. More recently, these structures have been adapted for learning-based 3D representations, such as NerfTree, which adopts a multistage, coarse-to-fine strategy similar to TNSVF [LGZL∗ 20] and Efficient-NeRF [HLC∗ 22] [S1].
+The Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) was introduced by Laine and Karras in 2010 as a method for efficient ray tracing of large volumetric datasets [LK10, cited in S1]. The concept of merging identical subtrees into DAGs for lossless compression was later developed to address the redundancy inherent in standard SVOs [898-bottom-up-svo-to-dag-reduction.md]. The Compact Pointer Tree was developed to handle terabyte-scale data by combining spatial locality with wavelet-based color compression [844-compact-pointer-tree.md]. More recently, NerfTree was proposed to accelerate Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by combining coarse density volumes with fine sparse voxels [S1].
 
